"""Append a finite DNA-event detection audit to the reviewed manuscript."""
import json,sys
from pathlib import Path
from docx import Document
from docx.oxml import OxmlElement
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];sys.path.insert(0,str(ROOT/'experiments'));import paper50 as P
p=ROOT/'paper/MEGA27-23b-50p.docx';d=Document(p);title='Single DNA substitutions can bypass the block checksum'
assert not any(x.text==title for x in d.paragraphs)
j=json.loads((ROOT/'results/block_checksum_dna_audit.json').read_text())
P.h1(d,title)
P.para(d,'A checksum counterexample in abstract trits could be dismissed as outside the DNA channel. We therefore enumerated actual changed-base events and passed them through the original differential decoder and low-byte block checksum. The deterministic 128-byte payload has byte i equal to (17i+31) modulo 256. We encode one copy with the current blocked codec and select the first eight payload blocks, spanning each of the four initial-base seeds twice. Each block has 32 payload trits and six check trits encoded as 38 bases. Selection is fixed, not representative sampling of every possible payload or checksum state.')
P.para(d,'For each selected block we enumerate every position and each of its three alternative DNA letters, then every pair of distinct positions and all nine pairs of alternative letters. Thus one block has 114 single-substitution and 6,327 double-substitution events. There are 912 and 50,616 events across eight blocks. Every event really changes the base; none retains the original letter. Each outcome is classified as invalid differential spacing, checksum rejection, accepted identical payload, or accepted changed payload. The original isolated block payload is the oracle, and the implementation acceptance function is also checked by a regression test.')
P.table(d,'Finite-event outcomes across eight fixed blocks. Counts are not biological read-error probabilities.',['Outcome','Single events','Double events'],[['Invalid spacing','490','39,407'],['Checksum reject','137','6,060'],['Accepted wrong payload','285','5,149'],['Accepted same payload','0','0'],['Total','912','50,616']])
P.h2(d,'Why one changed letter creates a checksum collision')
P.para(d,'Changing a base at position i changes the incoming and outgoing differential trits, if the outgoing position exists. When both resulting trits stay in the alphabet and their ordinary integer sum is unchanged, the low Fletcher accumulator s1 also stays unchanged. This is a common local cancellation mechanism, not a rare collision of an ideal random checksum. The full Fletcher pair additionally tracks cumulative position-weighted sums, so it can distinguish the retained example even though its low byte cannot. Invalid differential spacing rejects other substitutions first, which explains why the block detection rate cannot be inferred from checksum image size alone.')
P.para(d,'In block zero the first original bases are T,C,G with initial seed A. Replacing the first T by G changes the first decoded payload trits from [2,1] to [1,2]; later trits are unchanged. Both trits are valid, their sum remains three, and the six stored checksum trits still match. The isolated block accepts a wrong payload after just one changed DNA letter. The full twelve-trit Fletcher checksum differs, as verified by the new test. This establishes a concrete reachable miss, not a claim that the complete replicated message decoder returns a wrong file for this event.')
P.h2(d,'Event counts depend on region and sequence')
P.para(d,'All 285 single-event accepted wrong payloads occur at payload-region positions, out of 768 enumerated payload-position events; the 144 check-region events are rejected by spacing or checksum. The full 912-event accepted-wrong fraction is 31.25%, while the payload-only fraction is 37.11%. These are different denominators. Across individual blocks, single accepted-wrong counts range from 29 to 46 out of 114. A single aggregate fraction therefore should not be advertised as a universal conditional miss probability.')
P.para(d,'Among double events, 5,142 accepted wrong payloads have both changes in payload positions and seven have one payload and one check-position change. None has both changes only in the check region. Double-event accepted-wrong counts range from 427 to 1,019 per block, and the overall fraction is 5,149/50,616, approximately 10.17%. Its lower value than the single-event fraction does not mean that higher physical noise improves detection or recovery: the enumeration conditions on exactly two distinct changed positions, and more events fail the differential-spacing check. It does not mix event orders with their probabilities under a read model.')
P.h2(d,'Consequences for the proposed decoder and future design')
P.para(d,'The parity repair formula assumes detected erasures and correct remaining blocks. The present checksum can instead accept a wrong block, so parity alone is not an unconditional correction guarantee. In the replicated pipeline, a correct strict majority can remove a changed base before this check; in the complete message, the full Fletcher checksum can reject a corruption that passes the block check. These layers must be measured jointly, and no whole-file error rate follows from the isolated-block counts. A valid improvement would require a tested block code or checksum with explicit detection properties, complete length accounting, and a clean/noisy recovery sweep. It must also preserve a distinction between detecting corruption and correcting it.')
P.para(d,'No production encoder or decoder behavior is changed here. Replacing six check trits with the full Fletcher pair would add six bases per block and change density, screening and parity accounting; adding a stronger inner code introduces another tradeoff. Those are proposed experiments, not current results. The near-matched external Fountain sweep already shows that allocating more inner parity can reverse a codec ranking. The block-check audit adds a mechanistic reason to compare real detection and correction layers rather than an idealized checksum collision probability.')
P.h2(d,'Reproduction and limits')
P.para(d,'Run python3 experiments/block_checksum_dna_audit.py. The full JSONL event ledger includes block, event order, changed positions, new letters and outcome for every event. Its SHA256 digest is split below for legibility; concatenating the two lines restores the exact digest. The JSON summary retains region and distance breakdowns, exact payload identity and reproducible counterexamples. tests/test_block_checksum_dna_audit.py checks event totals, ledger integrity, the single-base reachable collision and agreement with the original acceptance function.')
P.para(d,j['trial_file_sha256'][:32]+'\n'+j['trial_file_sha256'][32:])
P.para(d,'Limits: eight fixed blocks are a finite library, not a random sample over all messages. The analysis fixes substitution count and treats only within-block changes. Indels, seam-spanning error coupling, physical synthesis, read consensus, whole-file correctness and biological feasibility are not validated here. The result withdraws the uniform block-collision and guaranteed-erasure assumptions; it does not invalidate the already retained empirical complete-file trials or establish a new universal bound.')
for t in d.tables[-1:]:
 for row in t.rows:row._tr.get_or_add_trPr().append(OxmlElement('w:cantSplit'))
P.save(d,str(p));print('appended DNA-event checksum audit')
